<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Two Treatises of Government</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1689">1689</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Two Treatises of Government is the principal work of political philosophy of the English philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>, published anonymously in 1689 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> but likely drafted in 1680-81 during the Exclusion Crisis. The First Treatise refutes, sentence by sentence, Robert Filmer's Patriarcha, which argued for a divinely ordained, hereditary, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>. The Second Treatise sets out a society based on natural rights and contract theory, in which government requires the consent of the governed and, under limited circumstances, the people may overthrow the ruler. The book is a key foundational text in the theory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/two-treatises-of-government.jpg" alt="Two Treatises of Government" width="330" height="506" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locke_treatises_of_government_page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
